    "title": "Part II — Sell the Transformation, Not the Tool",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Part II — Turn the Problem Into an Offer\nChapter 2: Sell the Transformation, Not the Tool\nCustomers rarely want a tool for its own sake. They want the result the tool helps them achieve.",
      "A business owner does not necessarily want “AI automation.” They may want fewer missed enquiries, faster response times, less repetitive administration, or a more reliable process. A creator may not want “video editing.” They may want consistent publishing without spending every evening editing clips.",
      "The tool is part of the delivery mechanism. The offer is the customer-facing solution.\nDefine the transformation\nWrite the offer as a movement from a current state to a desired state:\n- Current state: What is happening now?\n- Desired state: What should be happening instead?\n- Mechanism: How will your product or service help create the change?\n- Evidence: How will the customer know progress has been made?\nExample:\n| Weak description | Stronger outcome-led description |\n| --- | --- |\n| “I build AI chatbots.” | “I help service businesses answer common enquiries and capture qualified leads outside business hours.” |\n| “I edit short videos.” | “I turn existing long-form recordings into a consistent weekly batch of ready-to-publish short videos.” |\n| “I do marketing.” | “I help a defined type of business create and follow up with a steady flow of qualified enquiries.” |",
      "The stronger descriptions identify a buyer, a problem, and an outcome. They still need validation, but they give the prospect something concrete to evaluate."
    ]
