    "id": "chapter-7",
    "number": 7,
    "title": "Part IV — The Job of a Sales Conversation & Discovery Mindset",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Part IV — Sell Through Questions\nChapter 7: The Job of a Sales Conversation\nSales is not a performance in which the seller talks continuously and tries to defeat every objection. The seller's job is to understand the buyer's situation, determine whether the problem is real and important, and guide the buyer toward a suitable decision.",
      "A useful mental model is a staircase:\n- At the bottom is the buyer's current reality: the frustration, cost, delay, or missed outcome.\n- At the top is the result they want.\n- Between the two are the obstacles that must be addressed.\n- Your offer is a possible path across those steps.\nDifferent buyers are at different points. Some need to clarify the problem. Others already understand it and need to evaluate implementation, trust, budget, or timing.",
      "Do not force every prospect through the same pitch. Ask questions, listen, and respond to the situation in front of you.\nQualify before you sell\nA prospect is not automatically a good customer because they express interest. Consider:\n- Do they actually experience the problem?\n- Is solving it important to them?\n- Is there a realistic path to the desired outcome?\n- Can they afford the solution?\n- Are they involved in the decision?\n- Is the timing appropriate?\n- Can you deliver what they need?\n- Are their expectations reasonable?\nIf the fit is poor, say so. A sale that creates a bad outcome can cost more than the revenue is worth."
    ]
