  {
    "id": "chapter-19",
    "number": 19,
    "title": "Build Trust as the Business Grows: Reputation & Ethical Standards",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Chapter 19: Build Trust as the Business Grows\nA business that aims to scale needs more than persuasive marketing. It needs trust.",
      "Build trust through:\n- Accurate claims.\n- Clear pricing and scope.\n- Honest timelines.\n- Secure handling of customer information.\n- Reliable communication.\n- Transparent reporting.\n- Documented processes.\n- Consistent delivery.\n- Appropriate refunds and remedies.\n- Respect for customer choice.\nDo not invent case studies, testimonials, client logos, revenue numbers, or performance claims. If you are new, say so. Use a small pilot, a demonstration, a sample deliverable, or a clearly labelled example to show how you work.\nCreate proof from real work\nWith customer permission, document:\n1. The starting problem.\n2. The agreed scope.\n3. The work completed.\n4. The measurable result.\n5. The limitations and context.\n6. What the customer said, quoted accurately.\nA useful case study is specific and transparent. It explains what happened for that customer, not what every future customer is guaranteed to achieve."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "chapter-20",
    "number": 20,
    "title": "Part VIII — Worksheets, Operating Templates & Final $0-to-$1M Principles",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Part VIII — Worksheets and Templates\nWorksheet 1: Four-Circle Opportunity Finder\nComplete each section with specific examples.\nWhat I enjoy\n-\n-\nWhat I can do well\n-\n-\nProblems people need solved\n-\n-\nProblems people may pay to solve\n-\n-\nPotential overlap\n| Problem hypothesis | Customer | Evidence of pain | Existing alternative | What I still need to validate |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  |",
      "Worksheet 2: Problem Validation\nTarget customer:\nProblem:\nWhen it occurs:\nHow often it occurs:\nCurrent workaround:\nCost or consequence:\nWhat has already been tried:\nWho approves spending:\nEvidence that the problem is urgent:\nEvidence still missing:\nSmallest paid test:\nWorksheet 3: Offer Builder\nWho I help:\nProblem I solve:\nDesired transformation:\nMy method:\nMain deliverables:\nTimeline:\nCustomer responsibilities:\nExclusions:\nSuccess measures:\nRisk reversal or guarantee:\nBonus 1:\nBonus 2:\nBonus 3:\nHonest reason to act now:\nPrice:\nDelivery cost:\nExpected gross margin:\nWorksheet 4: Prospect Qualification\nScore each factor from 1 to 5, where 1 is weak and 5 is strong. The score is a thinking aid, not a substitute for judgement.\n| Factor | Score | Evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Problem fit |  |  |\n| Urgency |  |  |\n| Ability to pay |  |  |\n| Access to decision-maker |  |  |\n| Delivery fit |  |  |\n| Timing |  |  |",
      "Decision: pursue / nurture / disqualify\nReason:\nNext action:\nWorksheet 5: Discovery Call\nSetup\n- What do I know from verified public information?\n- What should I avoid assuming?\nCustomer\n- Who do they serve?\n- How does the current process work?\nDecision\n- Why are they looking at this now?\n- Who is involved?\nResults\n- What would success look like?\n- How will it be measured?\nReality\n- What is happening today?\n- What is the impact?\nRoadblocks\n- What has prevented progress?\n- What concerns remain?\nModel\n- What method is relevant to this situation?\nOffer\n- What scope, timeline, and price match the need?\nDecision\n- Is there a fit?\n- What is the agreed next step?\nWorksheet 6: Customer Onboarding\nCustomer:\nAgreed outcome:\nCurrent state:\nScope:\nDeliverables:\nExclusions:\nCustomer inputs required:\nAccess and permissions:\nFirst-value milestone:\nTarget date:\nCommunication cadence:\nSuccess measures:\nRisks and dependencies:\nWorksheet 7: Weekly Scorecard\n| Metric | This week | Previous week | What changed? |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Qualified prospects researched |  |  |  |\n| Personalised outreach sent |  |  |  |\n| Replies |  |  |  |\n| Discovery calls |  |  |  |\n| Qualified opportunities |  |  |  |\n| Proposals |  |  |  |\n| New customers |  |  |  |\n| Revenue collected |  |  |  |\n| Direct delivery cost |  |  |  |\n| Time to first value |  |  |  |\n| Customer issues |  |  |  |",
      "Biggest bottleneck:\nEvidence behind that diagnosis:\nOne change to test next week:\nOne thing to stop doing:\nFinal Principles: The Zero-to-$1M Plan\nThe process is not a secret trick or a guaranteed shortcut. It is a sequence of commercial disciplines:\n1. Find a painful problem. Interest is useful, but customer demand is essential.\n2. Validate with real people. A plausible idea is not a validated business.\n3. Make the offer clear. Buyers should understand the outcome, scope, price, and next step.\n4. Price for sustainable delivery. A business cannot improve if every sale creates an unprofitable obligation.\n5. Find buyers deliberately. Research, relevance, and respectful outreach beat indiscriminate volume.\n6. Sell through questions. Understand the buyer before recommending a solution.\n7. Qualify honestly. Do not sell to people you cannot help.\n8. Deliver value quickly. The customer's experience after payment determines whether trust grows.\n9. Use AI as leverage. Let it accelerate research, drafting, practice, and repeatable work; verify its output.\n10. Act before everything feels perfect. Small market-facing experiments create evidence that preparation alone cannot.\n11. Measure the business honestly. Revenue, profit, cash flow, and personal wealth are different.\n12. Improve the bottleneck. Build a repeatable process before trying to scale it.\nThe first objective is not to look like a large company. It is to solve one real problem for one real customer, get paid, and deliver what was promised. Then repeat the process, improve the offer, build a reliable acquisition system, and make delivery more consistent.",
      "A $1 million outcome—if it is the right target for the business—must be built from many smaller, verifiable customer outcomes. Start with the next one."
    ]
  }
